{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-05-special-ratio-transformations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Compound, Duplicate, and Transformed Ratios",
    "description": "Use the named ratio transformations in the chapter and see each one as a simple operation on both terms.",
    "teachingApproach": "Introduce every named transformation numerically before giving its symbolic name. Keep the focus on what happens to both terms, then combine transformations and use componendo-dividendo as a shortcut for sum/difference forms.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13, Ratio and Proportion. Approved scope: Teach the chapter's named ratio transformations: compounded ratio, reciprocal ratio, duplicate and sub-duplicate ratios, triplicate and sub-triplicate ratios, and componendo and dividendo. Build each from a numerical ratio before using symbols. Include geometric-chain relations x:y=y:z=z:u and identity-style questions where a transformed ratio is easier than solving individual values. Make clear that squaring or cubing applies to both terms."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Some ratio questions do not ask you to scale a ratio—they ask you to transform it.\n\nWe’ll separate compound, duplicate, sub-duplicate, triplicate, reciprocal, and related transformations so each move has a clear meaning.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A compounded ratio multiplies the first terms together and the second terms together.\n\nFor 2:3 and 5:7:\ncompound ratio = 10:21.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "With three ratios, do the same:\n(a:b), (c:d), (e:f) -> ace:bdf.\n\nIt is just multiplication across matching sides.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
